Code :

class Test {

    void c() {
        int x = 10 / 0;   // Exception occurs here
    }

    void b() {
        c();
    }

    void a() {
        b();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t = new Test();
        t.a();
    }
}
--------------------------------
What happens step by step?

main() calls a().
a() calls b().
b() calls c().
In c(), 10/0 causes an ArithmeticException.

Now ask yourself:

Does c() handle the exception? ❌ No.

So Java sends the exception back to the method that called c(), which is b().

Does b() handle it? ❌ No.

Java sends it to a().

Does a() handle it? ❌ No.

Java sends it to main().

Does main() handle it? ❌ No.

Finally, the JVM prints the exception and the program stops.

This movement of the exception from c() → b() → a() → main() is called exception propagation.
------------------------------
Now let's catch it

Code :

class Test {

    void c() {
        int x = 10 / 0;
    }

    void b() {
        c();
    }

    void a() {
        try {
            b();
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("Exception handled in a()");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t = new Test();
        t.a();
    }
}
--------------------------
As soon as a() catches it, the propagation stops. It does not go to main().
